      heading: "Tacrolimus labels: Prograf 5.16, Astagraf XL 5.14, Envarsus XR 5.14",
      body: "\u2022 The three labels print the same paragraph, with the product name: \"Cases of thrombotic microangiopathy (TMA), including hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) and thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP), have been reported in patients treated with [PRODUCT]. TMA may have a multifactorial etiology. Risk factors for TMA that can occur in transplant patients include, for example, severe infections, graft-versus-host disease (GVHD), Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) mismatch, the use of calcineurin inhibitors and mammalian target of rapamycin (mTOR) inhibitors. These risk factors may, either alone or combined, contribute to the risk of TMA.\" [1,2,3]\n\u2022 \"In patients with signs and symptoms of TMA, consider tacrolimus as a risk factor\" (Prograf, Astagraf XL); Envarsus XR: \"consider ENVARSUS XR as a risk factor\". \"Concurrent use of tacrolimus and mTOR inhibitors may contribute to the risk of TMA\" (Envarsus XR: \"of ENVARSUS XR and mTOR inhibitors\"). [1,2,3]\n\u2022 Prograf only, 5.10, Not Recommended for Use with Sirolimus: \"The use of sirolimus with PROGRAF may increase the risk of thrombotic microangiopathy\". [1]\n\u2022 Highlights (all three): TMA \"May occur, especially in patients with infections and certain concomitant medications\". [1,2,3]\n\u2022 Section 17.9, patient counselling (all three): \"Inform patients that [PRODUCT] can cause blood clotting problems. The risk of this occurring increases when patients take [PRODUCT] and sirolimus or everolimus concomitantly, or when patients develop certain infections. Advise them to seek medical attention promptly if they develop fever, petequiae or bruises, fatigue, confusion, jaundice, oliguria.\" [1,2,3]\n\u2022 The tacrolimus TMA sections print no action for TMA beyond considering the drug a risk factor (and the counselling above). [1,2,3]",
